---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ChapterNav, { type NavLink } from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
import H2 from '../components/headings/H2.astro';
import FullDemo from '../components/qr/FullDemo.svelte';
import { getChapters } from '../lib/chapters';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const last = (await getChapters()).at(-1);
const prev: NavLink | undefined = last && {
  href: url(last.id),
  label: `Chapter ${last.data.order}: ${last.data.title}`,
};
---

<Base
  title="Full demo"
  description="Make a QR code, damage it, and see whether it can still be read."
  current="demo"
  wide
>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Full demo</h1>
    <p>
      Make a QR code, damage it, and see whether it can still be read. Each step a scanner takes is shown with its
      result, and links to the chapter that explains it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <FullDemo client:visible />

  <div class="measure">
    <H2 id="things-to-try">Things to try</H2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Flip modules one at a time in one corner of the data area, and watch the damaged bytes add up until a block fails.
        Then try the same at level H.
      </li>
      <li>
        Damage the format information around the top-left finder pattern. With more than 3 bits wrong, the scanner reads
        the other copy instead (<a href="../format/#two-copies">chapter 2</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Type a longer text, so the code has several blocks, and add a blot. Interleaving shares its bytes out among the
        blocks (<a href="../reed-solomon/#splitting-into-blocks-and-interleaving">chapter 5</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <em>Cause a misread</em> damages a block so that it lies closer to the block for a slightly different text, and the
        decoder corrects it to that one (<a href="../reed-solomon/#too-many-errors">chapter 5</a>). The scanner returns the
        wrong text without noticing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Turn off the marks and point your phone's camera at the screen. Scanners differ in the details, and a camera adds
        its own problems, so a real one won't always agree.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <H2 id="what-the-demo-leaves-out">What the demo leaves out</H2>
    <p>
      A real scanner starts with a photo. It finds the three finder patterns, works out the grid of modules from them and
      the timing and alignment patterns, and decides whether each module is dark or light (section 12). This demo starts
      from the grid itself, so those patterns can't be damaged here.
    </p>
    <p>
      The demo also treats every damaged byte as an error. A scanner that knows some modules are unreadable, say under a
      smudge, could treat their bytes as <a href="../reed-solomon/#erasures">erasures</a> and correct up to twice as many of
      them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ChapterNav prev={prev} />
</Base>

<style>
  /* The page is wide for the widget; text keeps to the usual reading width. */
  .measure {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
